"Sustainable" has been printed on enough brochures that it risks meaning nothing. The
owners asking the sharpest questions now want it demonstrated at the level of an actual
purchase order, not a mission statement.

## Certification, not claims

FSC-certified timber, OEKO-TEX textiles and low-VOC finishes are verifiable. A supplier's
own sustainability page is not. The paperwork trail now gets requested alongside the
sample, not after the fact.

## Designed to be repaired, not replaced

Contract furniture built with replaceable upholstery, refinishable veneers and
serviceable joinery costs more to specify and less to own — a renovation-cycle saving that
rarely shows up in the original budget line but shows up clearly ten years later.

## Local sourcing, where it genuinely reduces impact

Shipping distance is one input among several — a locally made item manufactured with
higher-impact processes is not automatically the greener choice. The honest version of this
conversation weighs the whole supply chain, not just the number of borders a container
crosses.

The properties getting real credit for sustainability are not the ones with the longest
list of claims. They are the ones that can produce the certificate when asked.
